# ibviewer workflow (Babu branch)

## This is NOT the Next.js you know

This version has breaking changes — APIs, conventions, and file structure may all
differ from your training data. Read the relevant guide in
`node_modules/next/dist/docs/` before writing any code. Heed deprecation notices.

## What to ask about, what not to

- **Never ask for confirmation on tool mechanics** — shell commands, MCP calls,
  file edits. The allow-list in `.cursor/cli.json` is the approval.
- **Do ask** when the answer is genuinely the user's to make and is not derivable
  from the repo: product or design intent, ambiguous UX tradeoffs, which of
  several valid approaches they want, or a destructive/hard-to-reverse git action
  (force-push, `reset --hard` on unbacked-up work, merging into `upstream`,
  deleting a branch) that was not explicitly requested in the moment.
- A prior approval does not carry forward. "Merge Babu into main" today does not
  imply the same for a future unrelated task — ask again for each fresh instance
  of a risky action.

## Branch rules

- **Develop on `Babu`, never directly on `main`.** Confirm where you are with
  `git rev-parse --abbrev-ref HEAD`; don't assume.
- Two remotes may exist: `origin` (the personal fork,
  `danielalejandreperez/ibviewer`) and `upstream` (`itsmeBablu/IBV-Viewer`, the
  shared repo). Default to reading and pushing against `origin` only.
- **Never push, merge, or fetch-and-fast-forward into `upstream`** (either
  `upstream/Babu` or `upstream/main`) unless explicitly asked in that moment —
  it is shared with other collaborators.
- **All changes stay on `Babu` until told otherwise.** Never merge `Babu` into
  `main` on either remote unless the user asks for that specific task. Use the
  `merge-babu` skill when they do.
- **After a merge into `main`**, reset `Babu` to match rather than reconciling a
  diverged branch — and only when asked.

## Development workflow

- **Keep the dev server running.** When starting work, check `localhost:3000`
  first. If it is already up, leave it. If not, launch `npm run dev` and keep it
  running for the rest of the session so changes are visible live. Don't kill it
  between tasks. The `start-project` skill automates this whole check.
- **Windows PowerShell 5.1**: `&&` and bash heredocs are not supported. Use
  separate tool calls, and pass multi-line commit or PR bodies via a file
  (`git commit -F <file>`).

## End-of-task loop

Run automatically, without asking, whenever a coding task is finished:

1. Run `npm run lint`. There is no `typecheck`, `test`, or `test:e2e` script in
   this repo yet — if one is added later, fold it into this loop rather than
   running raw `tsc` or test binaries ad hoc.
2. Commit and push `Babu` to `origin`.
3. If lint is clean: tell the user the branch is ready and stop. Do not open a PR
   or touch `main`/`upstream` unless asked.
4. If lint fails: review the failure, fix the code, and repeat from step 1.
   Pre-existing failures unrelated to your change are not yours to fix silently —
   confirm against a baseline and report them instead of expanding scope.

## Code exploration

Prefer the `codebase-memory-mcp` tools (`search_graph`, `trace_path`,
`get_code_snippet`, `query_graph`, `get_architecture`, `search_code`) over
Grep/Glob for code-structure questions — they answer from a pre-built graph.
Still use Grep/Glob/Read for plain text, config, and non-code files, and always
read a file before editing it. If graph results disagree with what is on disk,
`delete_project` then `index_repository` for a clean rebuild.
